; A nullable (`T / null` -> Option<T>) sitting at a wasm position that adds its OWN presence-Option:
; a MAP VALUE (`get`/`insert` return Option<value>) and an OPTIONAL struct FIELD (getter returns
; Option<field>). Both would yield Option<Option<T>>, which wasm-bindgen can't represent
; (`Option<T>: OptionIntoWasmAbi` is not satisfied). The generated wasm accessors flatten to a single
; Option<T> — absent and present-null both read as None on the getter alone — mirroring the c-style
; enum-getter convention. The lost distinction is RESTORED additively (no getter signature changes) by
; presence accessors emitted from exactly this flatten condition: `has_field0()` beside the struct-field
; getter and map `has(key)` beside `get` (see the snapshots). Native storage keeps all three states
; (absent / present-null / present-value), so CBOR round-trips are unaffected. The behavioural oracle
; asserting three-state distinguishability through the wasm accessors is `tests/nullable-wasm/`; read
; protocols are in docs/docs/wasm_differences.mdx.
maybe_uint = uint / null
nullable_map_value = { * uint => maybe_uint }
nullable_optional_field = [pre: uint, ? field0: maybe_uint]

; Nullable of a COMPOSITE inner (a named collection / a data-enum), not just a primitive. The wasm
; boundary must map THROUGH the Option (the inner needs a wrapper conversion): a blanket `.into()`
; has no `From<Option<Inner>>` and the rust serialize must not double-return `serializer`. These
; exercise the struct-field, map-value and array-element roles for a composite nullable inner.
nums_arr = [* uint]
data_enum = uint / text
maybe_coll = nums_arr / null
maybe_denum = data_enum / null
nullable_coll_field = [c: maybe_coll]
nullable_denum_field = [d: maybe_denum]
nullable_coll_map = { * uint => maybe_coll }
